          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not inherently. Black mold usually refers to Stachybotrys chartarum, but many molds look dark, and CDC says identifying the species is not necessary. Remediation is priced by area, location and materials, not by mold type. Be skeptical of companies that use the term black mold to justify higher prices or urgent decisions."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does mold remediation take?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Small jobs are often done in a day or two. A typical basement or crawl space job may take two to five days including setup, removal, cleaning and drying. Large or whole-house projects can take a week or more, plus rebuilding time. Drying equipment and clearance testing can add a few days before walls are closed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I stay in my house during mold remediation?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a small, well-contained job, many people stay home, since containment and negative air keep spores in the work area. For large projects, or if anyone has asthma, allergies, a weakened immune system or other health concerns, talk to your doctor and ask the contractor whether temporary relocation is advisable."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
