    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Professional mold remediation typically costs roughly 1,500 to 6,000 dollars for a common job such as a section of basement, a bathroom or part of a crawl space, while a small contained area can come in under 1,500 and extensive attic, HVAC or whole-house work can run past 10,000. Per square foot of affected area, pros commonly charge somewhere around 10 to 25 dollars, with the total driven by access, containment and how much material has to be torn out. Those are typical US ranges that vary by region and change over time. Before you get quotes, know two things. EPA guidance says that if the moldy area is less than about 10 square feet, in most cases you can clean it yourself. And no remediation lasts unless the moisture that caused the mold is fixed first.
        </p>

        <div id="cost-table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Much Does Mold Remediation Cost by Area?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size is the starting point, but location and materials decide most of the price. A patch of mold on a concrete basement wall can be cleaned in place. The same area on drywall usually means cutting out and replacing the drywall, plus the insulation behind it. Mold in an attic means working on sheathing in a cramped, hot space. Contractors price all of that into what looks like a simple per-square-foot figure, which is why two jobs of the same size can have very different bills.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most companies quote a project total rather than a strict per-foot rate, and many have minimum charges because setting up containment, equipment and protective gear costs the same whether the area is small or moderate. That minimum is why a 12 square foot job can cost almost as much as a 40 square foot one. If your problem is genuinely small, the EPA's do-it-yourself guidance below may save you the whole fee.
          </p>

          <p>
            The table below shows typical ranges for common situations. Use it to sanity-check a quote, not as a price list. Regional labor rates, how hard the area is to reach, the amount of tear-out and rebuilding, and whether the water problem is fixed as part of the job all move the final number. Rebuilding finishes such as new drywall, paint and flooring is often quoted separately.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
